          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Parmenides of Elea</strong><br>\nParmenides was an ancient Greek philosopher born around 515 BCE in Elea, a city in southern Italy. He is one of the most significant pre-Socratic philosophers and is known for his work on metaphysics, which is the study of existence, reality, and the nature of being. Unlike others before him, Parmenides focused on the concept of being as a singular, unchanging entity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nLittle is known about the early life of Parmenides, but it is believed that he was a wealthy and noble figure in his society. His philosophical education was likely influenced by the work of Xenophanes and he may have been a student of Ameinias, a Pythagorean philosopher. Parmenides’ ideas were a significant departure from those of his predecessors and contemporaries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Poem of Parmenides</strong><br>\nParmenides expressed his philosophy through a poem titled \"On Nature,\" which only exists in fragments today. In it, he distinguishes between the way of truth, where he expounds his theory of being, and the way of opinion, where he discusses the beliefs of others that contrast with his own views. The poem has been central to the study of ancient philosophy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
