            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Way of Truth</strong><br>\nIn the \"way of truth\" part of his poem, Parmenides argues that reality is one, unchanging, and undivided. He challenges the notion of change and difference, stating that change is an illusion and that everything that exists is part of a single, unchanging whole. His work challenged the very foundations of what it means to exist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Concept of Being</strong><br>\nAccording to Parmenides, being is, and non-being is not. This simple yet profound statement laid the groundwork for metaphysics. He reasoned that we can only speak of what exists, and therefore, any discussion of non-being is impossible or nonsensical. This leads to the conclusion that change and plurality are mere illusions of the senses.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Illusion of Change</strong><br>\nParmenides' denial of change was radical. He argued that the sensory evidence of change and diversity among things was deceptive. True understanding, he maintained, comes from rational thought and not from unreliable sensory experiences. This line of thinking questioned the validity of the empirical observations that other philosophers relied upon.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
